Coventry and Warwickshire Pathology Services (“CWPS”) is seeking a partner to implement a dedicated Pathology Transport Service which will provide but not limited to, sample, consumable and chemical transport.
Coventry and Warwickshire Pathology Services (“CWPS”) is seeking a partner to implement a dedicated Pathology Transport Service which will provide sample and consumable transport, which may include chemicals.
CWPS is a managed network of pathology laboratories hosted by University Hospital Coventry and Warwickshire NHS Trust. Laboratory services are provided by laboratories at:
• University Hospital Coventry;
• South Warwickshire Hospital, Warwick; and
• George Eliot Hospital, Nuneaton; 
This procurement will provide a Service which will support pathology and phlebotomy services at 3 Trusts, which includes covering a population of 1.6 million patients and 242 phlebotomy sites.
The Service must provide for the safe transportation of:
a) Patient samples;
b) Blood and blood products;
c) Reagents and chemicals;
d) Consumables (including, but not limited to, blood collection tubes, sample collection vessels); and
e) Staff (between Main Sites). Transport of staff will only be required on an ad hoc basis and will not be routine movement of large numbers of staff.

First Garden Cities Homes (FGCH), a Registered Provider of Social Housing based in Letchworth Garden City and Welwyn Garden City, Hertfordshire, seeks a firm to act as its outsourced internal audit function from 1 April 2027. The appointed firm will report to FGCH's Audit & Risk Committee and provide the following:
•	risk-based strategic and annual internal audit plans;
•	delivery of the agreed programme of reviews;
•	follow-up of agreed actions through a recommendation tracker;
•	attendance at Audit & Risk Committee meetings;
•	support to counter-fraud arrangements;
•	an annual internal audit opinion.
The contract is rate-based. Tenderers price a day rate for each grade against a grade mix set by FGCH, which produces a composite day rate fixed for years 1 to 3. The Audit & Risk Committee commissions the volume of work each year; the planning assumption is 50 days a year for years 1 to 3. Delivery is hybrid: predominantly remote, with up to 10% of days on site. The initial term is three years, from 1 April 2027 to 31 March 2030, with an option to extend once by two years.
Procedure description
Competitive Flexible Procedure under section 20 of the Procurement Act 2023, run in two assessed stages.
•	Stage 1 (written tender): open to all suppliers. It comprises the Procurement Specific Questionnaire (pass/fail), four written quality questions (55%) and the composite day rate (30%). Two thresholds apply: a tender scoring 0 or 1 on any quality question is excluded, as is a tender scoring less than 57.5% of the quality marks available.
•	Intermediate assessment: under s.20(4)(a) and (5)(b), the two highest-ranked tenderers proceed, or more where there is a tie for second place.
•	Stage 2 (evaluated dialogue, 15%): held with an Audit & Risk Committee panel on 27 January 2027 via Microsoft Teams.
There is no negotiation, and tenders and fees may not be revised after the tender deadline. Full details are in the tender documents (Appendix A).
Reasons for not dividing into lots
Internal audit is a single, integrated function: one audit universe, one annual plan, one recommendation tracker and one annual opinion on FGCH's framework of governance, risk management and control. Dividing it into lots would fragment that opinion, duplicate planning and reporting, and remove the single point of accountability to the Audit & Risk Committee.
Award criteria
•	Quality, 55%: Q1 to Q4 at 13.75% each.
•	Fee (composite day rate), 30%.
•	Evaluated dialogue, 15%: criteria P1 to P3 at 5% each.
Conditions of participation (summary)
Tenderers must:
•	not be FGCH's statutory external auditor, and no entity in the firm or network may provide statutory audit to FGCH;
•	confirm independence and disclose any services provided to FGCH in the last five years;
•	confirm conformance with the Global Internal Audit Standards, with an external quality assessment completed in the last five years;
•	hold the insurance cover set out in the ITT;
•	demonstrate current experience of internal audit for English Registered Providers of Social Housing.

Kent County Council acts as accountable body for the Kent & Medway Business Fund and manages a portfolio of business investment schemes. These schemes provide loan and investment funding to eligible businesses, supporting business growth, an increase in productivity, innovation and employment across Kent and Medway.
Independent financial appraisal acts as an essential control within the Fund’s investment process. The provider will:
•undertake financial appraisal of new loan applications;
•assess the financial position and viability of applicants;
•review requests for changes to existing lending arrangements where required;
•advise on repayment capacity, security, conditions and financial risk;
•support consideration of applications by the relevant decision-making body;
•attend Investment Advisory Board and related meetings; and
•undertake associated financial appraisal and advisory work.
Kent County Council (KCC) is looking for a contractor to provide independent financial appraisal reports to support the investment decisions of the KCC managed business investment schemes, to include:
a)to attend to 2-3 decision meetings each month on request; 
b)to prepare at the full application stage for each eligible applicant of the KCC managed business investment schemes, a financial appraisal report. This report should contain business and financial analysis of the investment proposal and be submitted via KCC to the monthly decision meetings;
c)to prepare at the debt recovery stage for existing loan recipients of KCC managed business investment schemes, a financial appraisal report. This report should contain business and financial analysis of the current financial position of the business to assist in the understanding of the current financial position of a company. This report to be submitted via KCC to the monthly decision meeting; 
d)each financial appraisal report should include a range of information to assist the monthly decision-making meeting make its Recommendations i.e. to Partially Fund/Fully Fund/Reject/Defer, suggest conditions, repayment schedule and security (if funding was to be offered); 
e)there may be an ad-hoc requirement for follow-up independent appraisal work connected with the KCC managed business investment schemes.
The contract is anticipated to commence on 1st February 2027 and will continue for a period of 3 year(s) (36 months) unless terminated in accordance with the Conditions of Contract.  There will be an optional 12 month extension period, subject to agreement by the Council.

