The University of Salford is undertaking preliminary market engagement to inform a potential procurement for the provision of a digital solution to support the management and automation of Confirmation of Acceptance for Studies (CAS) issuance and associated credibility and compliance processes for prospective international students.
The University is seeking to understand the solutions available in the market that can support an efficient, transparent and compliant applicant journey from the point at which an international applicant receives an offer through to CAS issuance and, where appropriate, subsequent stages of the visa process.
The anticipated requirement is for an established, configurable solution capable of supporting and automating the University's CAS assessment and issuance processes. This is expected to include:
•	workflow management for applicants progressing towards CAS-ready status;
•	collection, submission, review and management of applicant information and supporting documentation;
•	assessment and recording of relevant checks associated with the University's international student sponsorship responsibilities and UK Visas and Immigration (UKVI) requirements;
•	applicant, agent and University staff visibility of case status, progress and outstanding requirements;
•	functionality to request additional information or documentation through the platform, reducing reliance on email and manual intervention;
•	scheduling and management of pre-CAS credibility interviews and assessments;
•	facilities for conducting, recording and subsequently reviewing online credibility interviews;
•	functionality to support the identification of potential impersonation and inappropriate use of artificial intelligence or other technologies during credibility assessment processes;
•	recording and analysis of visa outcomes, including visa refusals;
•	tracking of applicants through relevant stages of the visa process;
•	management information, reporting and data analytics to support regulatory compliance, operational oversight, international recruitment and applicant conversion; and
•	appropriate information management, access controls and auditability to support University processes.
Through this procurement, the University intends to strengthen its ability to manage its international student sponsorship responsibilities effectively while improving the experience of applicants, agents and University colleagues.
The University wishes to engage with interested suppliers before determining its final specification and procurement approach. The preliminary market engagement is intended to improve the University's understanding of:
•	the range and maturity of solutions currently available;
•	the extent to which the anticipated requirements can be met through standard, off-the-shelf functionality and where configuration may be required;
•	approaches to integration with relevant University systems and processes;
•	reporting, management information and analytical capabilities; and
•	commercial and licensing models available within the market.
At this stage, the University is not able to confirm the anticipated contract value, contract duration or procurement and implementation timescales. These will be informed by the preliminary market engagement, including the University's understanding of the solutions available, commercial and licensing models, implementation approaches and associated timescales. Any indicative values or dates included in this notice should therefore be treated as provisional and do not constitute a commitment by the University.
The University may use information obtained through this engagement to develop its requirements, specification, commercial model, anticipated contract value and duration, implementation timescales and procurement strategy.

A comprehensive MDR service to support the protection of its users, information, systems and technology environment against cyber security threats.
A managed cyber security service providing 24 hours a day, 7 days a week monitoring, detection, investigation and response capability, supported by appropriate security expertise, processes and technologies.
The anticipated scope includes:
24/7 Security Operations Centre (SOC) monitoring and response;
Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) and/or Extended Detection and Response (XDR);
Integration with and monitoring of Microsoft 365 and Microsoft Entra ID;
Identity-based threat detection;
Security alert investigation, triage and prioritisation;
Proactive threat hunting;
Threat intelligence;
Incident containment and remediation support;
Incident escalation and notification;
Digital forensics and incident investigation;
Integration with CHS's existing security and technology environment;
Onboarding and deployment;
Security reporting and service reviews;
Vulnerability and exposure insights;
Access to security specialists and escalation resources;
Support during significant cyber security incidents; and
Continual service improvement.
Details of the following would also be beneficial:
MDR service and operating models, including the division of responsibilities between CHS and the provider.
Technologies deployed or integrated with in delivering MDR services.
Integration with Microsoft 365, Microsoft Entra ID and other relevant security technologies.
Threat detection, investigation, proactive threat hunting, containment and remediation capabilities.
Incident response arrangements, including support for serious or high-impact cyber incidents.
24/7 SOC operating models and SOC locations.
Relevant security standards, certifications and independent assurance.
Security data processing, retention, protection and residency arrangements.
Typical severity classifications, response times and service levels.
Reporting, customer dashboards, service reviews and security metrics.
Onboarding, implementation and transition arrangements.
Typical licensing and commercial models.
CHS expects a combination of vulnerability and exposure capabilities: visibility of vulnerabilities and material exposures across endpoints, servers, identity and relevant cloud services; threat-informed and risk-based prioritisation; clear remediation advice and workflow; trend and exception reporting; and escalation of high-risk exposures. A full managed vulnerability-management or external attack-surface service may be proposed and separately priced where it adds value, but bidders must distinguish included MDR capability from optional services.
Requirement
Existing third-party security and management products that may require monitoring or integration include Sophos Endpoint / Intercept X and Sophos MDR, Sophos Mobile, Mimecast, Fortinet FortiGate, Meraki MX/Dashboard, the Zyxel site firewall, ManageEngine Endpoint Central, Microsoft 365 / Entra ID and Veeam Backup for Microsoft 365. Bidders should identify native integrations, any tier limits, additional connectors and any products that would be replaced rather than integrated.
All system users
CHS has approximately 211 employees. For technical sizing, assume approximately 180 Microsoft 365 / Entra ID licensed user identities, 171 Windows workstations (150 laptops and 21 desktops), 31 server assets (5 physical and 26 virtual), and 14 operational locations. These figures are approximate and will be confirmed during mobilisation.
The current planning baseline is approximately 171 Windows workstations (150 laptops and 21 desktops); 31 server assets (5 physical servers, including 4 Hyper-V hosts, plus 26 virtual machines); 114 managed mobile/tablet devices (113 Android and 1 iOS); and approximately 180 Microsoft 365 / Entra ID licensed user identities. The server estate comprises approximately 27 Windows servers/hosts and 4 Ubuntu Linux virtual machines. We do not currently have macOS endpoints in scope. These are planning figures and will be validated during mobilisation.
The principal cloud and SaaS environment is Microsoft 365 / Entra ID, with a limited Azure footprint. No AWS or GCP environment is currently identified as in scope. Relevant SaaS and management platforms include Sophos Central/MDR, Mimecast, ManageEngine Endpoint Central and ServiceDesk Plus, Meraki Dashboard, Veeam Backup for Microsoft 365, 3CX, Exclaimer, IRIS HR, CharityLog, OneAdvanced, TextAnywhere and the P2P finance platform. CHS has 14 operational locations. The main site includes server, SAN, wireless, management and DMZ segments; other locations principally include corporate, guest and/or resident networks. Bidders should propose the appropriate collector or network-sensor design and state any appliance, virtual machine, port or connectivity requirements.
CHS has not set a single fixed retention period for all log sources at this clarification stage. Bidders should state their standard hot/searchable and archive retention, explain what is included in the price, identify any legal/evidence-hold capability, and price practical extension options. The final source-by-source retention schedule will be agreed during service design, taking account of security, investigation, data-protection and cost requirements.
A reliable GB/day or GB/month figure is not currently available. Bidders should size their proposal from the disclosed users, assets, locations and expected integrations, state all ingestion assumptions and exclusions, and provide transparent pricing for included capacity and overage. Actual volumes will be validated during discovery before final onboarding of each source.
Integration
Initial integration scope should include Microsoft 365 / Entra ID; the on-premises Active Directory and DNS environment; Windows endpoints and Windows/Linux servers; Sophos Endpoint/MDR where retained; Mimecast; Fortinet FortiGate; Meraki MX/Dashboard; the Zyxel site firewall; ManageEngine Endpoint Central; Veeam Backup for Microsoft 365; VPN and authentication events; and relevant Azure activity. Other business-critical SaaS sources will be prioritised during discovery according to security value, technical feasibility and cost. No AWS or GCP environment is currently identified.
The successful supplier is expected to provide and implement the technologies and services needed to deliver the proposed MDR outcome. This may include the supplier's SIEM/SOC platform, EDR/XDR, vulnerability/exposure capability, threat intelligence, automation/SOAR and supporting collectors. CHS is open to retaining suitable existing tools or replacing them. The bidder must identify what is included, what remains CHS-owned, any third-party dependencies and licences, and the complete implementation and recurring cost.
CHS expects the successful bidder to integrate with Microsoft 365 / Entra ID, identity, email, endpoint, server and agreed network-security telemetry. Mimecast, Fortinet/Meraki, ManageEngine and Veeam should be considered for integration where technically appropriate. The existing Sophos endpoint/MDR tooling may be retained, replaced or used in controlled coexistence, depending on the bidder's proposed solution. Any replacement must include a safe migration, validation and rollback approach, with all costs included.
Contract Period - Pricing schedule (basis-annual/3/5 years and confirmation of any inflationary increases)
The pricing return will require transparent whole-life pricing for the three-year initial term and both optional one-year extensions, including implementation, migration, integrations, licences, telemetry/retention, support and exit.

